error[ffi_parameter_type]: `value` of `slot_read` is declared `f64` and crosses by value, and `fixedbugs-2421-variadic.h` gives `slot_read` 1 parameter and then `...`, where `value` stands — C reads what a call hands it there as whatever type it asks for, so nothing holds it to the `f64` this program hands it
  at tests/golden/unsupported/fixedbugs-2421-a-float-past-a-variadic-prototype-is-refused.hero:26:36
     |
  26 |     function slot_read(count: i32, value: f64) -> i64  #~ ffi_parameter_type
     |                                    ^^^^^
  note: spec § 13: a parameter is declared at the header's own width and sign, and clang holds it to them; past a header's `...` the header declares no type, so the call converts nothing beyond C's default promotions and `slot_read` reads the bytes as the type its `va_arg` names: an `i32` read as a C `long` gives -1 as 4294967295, and a wider integer, a float, a string, a pointer or a record read as another type is the same mistake
  note: write a header of your own that includes `fixedbugs-2421-variadic.h` and defines a `static inline` function of a new name with a fixed prototype, taking `value` at the C type `slot_read` reads there and calling `slot_read` with it; bind that function instead, and its prototype holds it to that type; where `slot_read` reads a format, write the format inside that function as a literal, and clang reads each conversion against the argument it is given

